{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "SuperFastDepositionSink",
    "description": "Operator model that turns first-order dry + wet deposition loss-rate coefficients into additive sink terms on a SuperFast-family gas-phase mechanism. Each equation is D(<X>) ~ -(k_dry + k_wet)*<X> over the model's OWN species unknowns; when this model is merged with a SuperFast reaction system via an `operator_compose` coupling (systems: [<Chem>, SuperFastDepositionSink]), the flattener matches each sink equation's dependent variable to the chemistry system's state of the same species name and sums the two right-hand sides (dc/dt += -k*c), then retires the sink's own declaration of that name (esm-spec 10.1/10.2 — direct match, else the bare-name match). The deposition rate coefficients are exposed as parameters so they can be driven from the full DryDepositionGas (Wesley 1989) and WetDeposition (EMEP) components via param_to_var edges in an assembly. Species->rate mapping mirrors jialinl6/DepositionMTK.jl@cbe7d7db (branch superfast-gas-only) ext/GasChemExt.jl couple2(SuperFastCoupler, DryDepositionGasCoupler / WetDepositionCoupler): NO is not dry-deposited (GEOS-Chem does not dry-deposit it, and upstream follows); CH2O uses the GEOS-Chem-aligned k_CH2O rate (Hstar 3e3, f0 1), not the Wesely-table k_HCHO, whose f0 = 0 made CH2O deposit ~15x too slowly; the wet rate k_wet is the lumped soluble-gas WetDeposition.k_othergas applied to HNO3, H2O2, CH2O, CH3OOH.\n\nThe species are declared here as ordinary unknowns with the upstream SuperFast initial mixing ratios as defaults, so the applicator is a complete, standalone-loadable, standalone-testable document: on its own it integrates six decoupled first-order decays whose closed-form solution c(t) = c0*exp(-k*t) is what the inline tests pin, and the species->rate mapping (k_wet only on the four soluble species, CH2O on k_CH2O) is asserted rather than only asserted in prose. Composition is unaffected: operator_compose matches on the species NAME.\n\nThis replaces an earlier spelling in which the equations were written directly against `SuperFast.<X>` scoped references. That form hard-coded the partner system's document-level name into the leaf and, per esm-spec 4.6 (the head segment of a scoped reference MUST name a key of this document's `models` / `reaction_systems`, or a subsystem of the referencing model), referenced a system this document does not declare — so `load_path` rejected the file with `reference '<X>' to undefined system 'SuperFast'` and neither the CI gate nor any runner could load it. Mounting SuperFast as a `subsystems` entry would have silenced that error only by changing the composition: the chemistry would then live INSIDE the sink at `SuperFastDepositionSink.SuperFast.*` and no longer merge with the sibling chemistry system under operator_compose. Name matching is the construct the spec provides for this, and it is what the flattener implements.\n\nIMPORTANT (esm implementation note, carried over from the migration): the additive sink is delivered as a model whose D(<species>) equations are merged by operator_compose, NOT via a `couple` connector with transform:\"additive\". The additive/from/to connector form is accepted by the schema (esm-spec 10.3) but was observed during migration not to reach the ODEs through the flatten pipeline's `_apply_couple!` (a {from,to,transform:additive,expression} edge went to opaque_refs and was discarded). operator_compose (which sums equations sharing a dependent variable) is the working construct.",
    "tags": ["atmospheric_deposition", "gaschem", "superfast", "deposition-sink", "operator-compose"],
    "references": [
      { "citation": "Wesely, M. L. (1989). Parameterization of surface resistances to gaseous dry deposition in regional-scale numerical models. Atmospheric Environment 23(6):1293-1304." }
    ]
  },
  "models": {
    "SuperFastDepositionSink": {
      "variables": {
        "O3":     { "type": "unknown", "units": "ppb", "default": 40.0,   "description": "Ozone mixing ratio the dry-deposition sink acts on. Merged onto the chemistry system's O3 by operator_compose; the default is SuperFast's own O3 initial value (40 ppb), used when this applicator is integrated standalone." },
        "NO2":    { "type": "unknown", "units": "ppb", "default": 0.0004, "description": "Nitrogen dioxide mixing ratio. Default is SuperFast's NO2 initial value." },
        "HNO3":   { "type": "unknown", "units": "ppb", "default": 4e-06,  "description": "Nitric acid mixing ratio. Soluble: loses to both dry deposition and wet scavenging. Default is SuperFast's HNO3 initial value." },
        "H2O2":   { "type": "unknown", "units": "ppb", "default": 4e-06,  "description": "Hydrogen peroxide mixing ratio. Soluble: dry + wet. Default is SuperFast's H2O2 initial value." },
        "CH2O":   { "type": "unknown", "units": "ppb", "default": 4e-06,  "description": "Formaldehyde mixing ratio. Soluble: dry (at the k_CH2O rate) + wet. Default is SuperFast's CH2O initial value." },
        "CH3OOH": { "type": "unknown", "units": "ppb", "default": 4e-06,  "description": "Methyl hydroperoxide mixing ratio. Wet scavenging only — GasChemExt gives it no dry-deposition rate. Default is SuperFast's CH3OOH initial value." },
        "k_O3":   { "type": "parameter", "units": "1/s", "default": 0.0, "description": "O3 dry-deposition first-order loss rate (from DryDepositionGas.k_O3)." },
        "k_NO2":  { "type": "parameter", "units": "1/s", "default": 0.0, "description": "NO2 dry-deposition rate. NO has no dry-deposition sink (upstream, after GEOS-Chem)." },
        "k_HNO3": { "type": "parameter", "units": "1/s", "default": 0.0, "description": "HNO3 dry-deposition rate." },
        "k_H2O2": { "type": "parameter", "units": "1/s", "default": 0.0, "description": "H2O2 dry-deposition rate." },
        "k_CH2O": { "type": "parameter", "units": "1/s", "default": 0.0, "description": "CH2O (formaldehyde) dry-deposition rate, from DryDepositionGas.k_CH2O." },
        "k_wet":  { "type": "parameter", "units": "1/s", "default": 0.0, "description": "Lumped soluble-gas wet-scavenging rate (from WetDeposition.k_othergas), applied to HNO3, H2O2, CH2O, CH3OOH." }
      },
      "equations": [
        { "lhs": { "op": "D", "args": ["O3"],     "wrt": "t" }, "rhs": { "op": "*", "args": [ { "op": "-", "args": ["k_O3"] }, "O3" ] } },
        { "lhs": { "op": "D", "args": ["NO2"],    "wrt": "t" }, "rhs": { "op": "*", "args": [ { "op": "-", "args": ["k_NO2"] }, "NO2" ] } },
        { "lhs": { "op": "D", "args": ["HNO3"],   "wrt": "t" }, "rhs": { "op": "*", "args": [ { "op": "-", "args": [ { "op": "+", "args": ["k_HNO3", "k_wet"] } ] }, "HNO3" ] } },
        { "lhs": { "op": "D", "args": ["H2O2"],   "wrt": "t" }, "rhs": { "op": "*", "args": [ { "op": "-", "args": [ { "op": "+", "args": ["k_H2O2", "k_wet"] } ] }, "H2O2" ] } },
        { "lhs": { "op": "D", "args": ["CH2O"],   "wrt": "t" }, "rhs": { "op": "*", "args": [ { "op": "-", "args": [ { "op": "+", "args": ["k_CH2O", "k_wet"] } ] }, "CH2O" ] } },
        { "lhs": { "op": "D", "args": ["CH3OOH"], "wrt": "t" }, "rhs": { "op": "*", "args": [ { "op": "-", "args": ["k_wet"] }, "CH3OOH" ] } }
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "dry_deposition_only_first_order_decay",
          "description": "Dry deposition alone (k_wet = 0), one hour. Each species obeys the closed-form first-order decay c(t) = c0*exp(-k*t), so every expected value below is analytic, not a recorded output: O3 = 40*exp(-1e-4*3600) = 40*exp(-0.36); NO2 = 4e-4*exp(-2e-4*3600) = 4e-4*exp(-0.72). CH3OOH is asserted UNCHANGED at its initial 4e-6 ppb, pinning that it carries no dry-deposition term. Tolerance rel=1e-6 is a numerical-integration band over 3600 s of a linear decay, well inside the runner's default accuracy and far tighter than the ~4e-2 relative change the mapping itself is being distinguished by.",
          "time_span": { "start": 0.0, "end": 3600.0 },
          "parameter_overrides": {
            "k_O3": 0.0001,
            "k_NO2": 0.0002,
            "k_HNO3": 0.0,
            "k_H2O2": 0.0,
            "k_CH2O": 0.0,
            "k_wet": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            { "variable": "O3",     "time": 3600.0, "expected": 27.90705304284124,      "tolerance": { "rel": 1e-06 } },
            { "variable": "NO2",    "time": 3600.0, "expected": 0.00019470090238398867, "tolerance": { "rel": 1e-06 } },
            { "variable": "CH3OOH", "time": 3600.0, "expected": 4e-06,                  "tolerance": { "rel": 1e-09 } }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "wet_scavenging_adds_only_to_soluble_species",
          "description": "Wet scavenging switched on (k_wet = 5e-5 1/s) alongside two dry rates, one hour. The four soluble species lose at (k_dry + k_wet) and the insoluble ones at k_dry alone, so the expected values are again analytic: HNO3 = 4e-6*exp(-(1e-4+5e-5)*3600) = 4e-6*exp(-0.54); H2O2 = 4e-6*exp(-5e-5*3600) = 4e-6*exp(-0.18) (no dry rate set, wet only); CH2O = 4e-6*exp(-(3e-5+5e-5)*3600) = 4e-6*exp(-0.288), which pins that CH2O rides the CH2O dry rate k_CH2O; CH3OOH = 4e-6*exp(-0.18), wet only. O3 is asserted UNCHANGED at 40 ppb with every dry rate zeroed, pinning that k_wet is NOT applied to the insoluble species — the half of the GasChemExt mapping that a wet-only assertion on the soluble four cannot distinguish.",
          "time_span": { "start": 0.0, "end": 3600.0 },
          "parameter_overrides": {
            "k_O3": 0.0,
            "k_NO2": 0.0,
            "k_HNO3": 0.0001,
            "k_H2O2": 0.0,
            "k_CH2O": 3e-05,
            "k_wet": 5e-05
          },
          "assertions": [
            { "variable": "HNO3",   "time": 3600.0, "expected": 2.3309930094959583e-06, "tolerance": { "rel": 1e-06 } },
            { "variable": "H2O2",   "time": 3600.0, "expected": 3.341080845645088e-06,  "tolerance": { "rel": 1e-06 } },
            { "variable": "CH2O",   "time": 3600.0, "expected": 2.9990463689561647e-06, "tolerance": { "rel": 1e-06 } },
            { "variable": "CH3OOH", "time": 3600.0, "expected": 3.341080845645088e-06,  "tolerance": { "rel": 1e-06 } },
            { "variable": "O3",     "time": 3600.0, "expected": 40.0,                   "tolerance": { "rel": 1e-09 } }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
